-### Vendor price scaling: high-level stock no longer sells at its own low base price (patch 0356)
+### New system: recipe drop/learn (WoW-style), Gems/Sigils/Charms/Gear/tiered Consumables now gated behind found scrolls (patch 0357)
+```discord-update
+Dev Update | Recipe Drop System
+
+- New system: most recipes now have to be found before they can be crafted, the same way World of Warcraft's recipe drops work
+- Every guild still starts knowing the basics -- Common gems, Minor Sigils, Lucky Charms, and a few starter potions/food
+- Everything else (higher gem tiers, better sigils/charms, every piece of Gear, and 6 tiered potions/food) drops from raids and quests -- harder content, better odds
+- New Recipes section in the Inventory tab, right above Curios, where a found-but-unlearned recipe scroll waits to be learned (or sold, if you'd rather cash in a duplicate)
+- Recipes you haven't found yet still show up in the Crafting list, greyed out, so you know what's out there to chase
+- If you already had a save going, nothing changes for you -- every recipe you already had access to stays unlocked
+```
+
+Direct design request, fleshed out over a few rounds of back-and-forth
+before any code: "let all the lowest tiers available at the start, and
+have the rest drop via recipe... like World of Warcraft's recipe drop/
+learn system." Net new system, not a bug fix -- no existing mechanic
+touched anything close to this before.
+
+**Scope, decided directly with the user rather than guessed.** Of the
+game's 72 crafting recipes, **56 are now gated**, split across every
+family that actually has a tier ladder (or, for Gear, doesn't but got
+gated individually anyway per direct request):
+- **Gems** (40 total, 8 element/type lines × 5 rarities) -- the
+  `common` tier of all 8 lines is free; uncommon/rare/epic/legendary (32)
+  are gated.
+- **Enchant Sigils** (3) -- Minor is free; Standard and Greater (2) are
+  gated.
+- **Charms** (6) -- Lucky Charm, gold and xp variants, are free;
+  Fortune Weave and Windfall Sigil (4) are gated.
+- **Gear** (12) -- all 12 individually gated, direct request ("each gear
+  recipe has its own individual scroll") rather than one flat unlock or
+  a free starting tier, since Gear has no natural tier ladder to anchor
+  a "lowest tier" on to begin with.
+- **Consumables** (11) -- 3 genuine 3-tier "food type" families were
+  found hiding in the actual data by cross-referencing effect magnitude,
+  not just recipe names: a Healing line (Herbal Tonic -> Alchemist's
+  Reserve -> Phoenix Ember), a Travel-food line (Trail Rations -> Meal
+  On The Go -> Warband Feast), and a Fortune line (Forager's Bundle ->
+  Prospector's Cache -> Dragon's Hoard Charm). Each line's tier-1 is
+  free, the other 6 are gated. Pet Treat and Beckoning Charm have no
+  tiered siblings at all and stay always-known.
+
+16 always-known + 56 gated == all 72 recipes, verified by script before
+writing a line of game code.
+
+**New data (`types.ts`, `data/json/recipe-scrolls.json`,
+`data/recipeScrolls.ts`).** `RecipeScrollDef` is deliberately thin --
+just `id`/`recipeId`/`reqLevel`/`rarity`, no name/description/icon of
+its own, since a scroll's display always derives from the
+`CraftingRecipeDef` it unlocks rather than duplicating that data a
+second time. Every scroll's `reqLevel`/`rarity` was pulled from the
+*real* underlying data (a gear scroll matches its item's own reqLevel/
+rarity; a gem/sigil/charm scroll follows its own tier), not hand-typed
+guesses -- generated by script, cross-checked against
+`crafting-recipes.json`/`equipment.json` before being written out.
+`GameState` gains two new fields: `recipeScrolls` (an owned-but-
+unlearned scroll's stock count, same open-ended "keyed by def id, no
+cap" shape `curios` already has) and `unlockedRecipes` (the permanent
+unlock list, a recipe id lands here once and never leaves).
+
+**New manager (`RecipeManager.ts`).** Mirrors `CurioManager`'s exact
+shape (`owned`/`add`/`sellAll`/`sellEverything`) for the "it's just
+inventory until you do something with it" half, plus what's actually
+new: `isKnown`, `learn` (consumes one scroll, permanently unlocks its
+recipe), and `eligibleDrops`/`rollDrop` (unweighted pick among every
+scroll whose `reqLevel` fits the content's own level AND isn't already
+known -- direct answer, "can't drop again if learnt" is enforced here,
+but an unused duplicate can still drop again and again until the recipe
+is actually learned).
+
+**Crafting gate (`CraftingManager.affordability`).** One line, checked
+first, ahead of gold/scrap/materials: `RecipeManager.isKnown`. This one
+function already fed all 4 craft-execution paths (gear/consumable/
+enchant/gem) and the UI's own Craft-button greying before this patch, so
+gating it there covers every category in a single change rather than
+four separate ones.
+
+**Drops (`QuestManager.resolve`, `RaidManager.resolve`).** Both direct
+answer: "both raids and quests." Deliberately NOT hand-authored loot
+tables per encounter (the way equipment/egg drops already are) -- a flat,
+independent, per-difficulty roll instead, same shape the existing curio/
+egg drops already use on the quest side. Quests roll on success, gated
+by `quest.offer.reqLevel`; raids roll once per full clear (not per
+encounter), gated by `raid.reqLevel`. New tuning: `quest.recipeDropChance.*`
+(1.5% Easy -> 9% Legendary) and `raid.recipeDropChance.*` (5% Normal ->
+22% Legendary) -- both direct answer, "harder content has higher chance
+to drop recipe." Both `QuestResult`/`RaidResult` gained a
+`recipeScrollGained` field, and both result modals now show it under a
+new "Also found" line (QuestResultModal already had one for eggs/
+curios/materials; RaidResultModal didn't have an equivalent section at
+all before this patch, so `eggsFound` -- which turned out to have never
+actually been wired into that modal either -- got fixed alongside the
+new recipe field rather than leaving it the one silently-broken
+"informational" field left in that result).
+
+**New Inventory section (`EquipmentPanel.tsx`).** "Recipes," directly
+above Curios per direct request, same footprint: a grid of owned scroll
+cards (`RecipeScrollCard`, mirroring `CurioCard`'s click-to-modal shape)
+plus a Sell All button. Each card has two actions instead of Curios'
+one -- Learn (permanent, consumes the scroll) and Sell (cashes in a
+duplicate or an unwanted find, direct answer: "they have a value, can
+sell dupes"). Sell All uses the same staggered "snapshot positions,
+animate each card individually toward the header's gold readout" shape
+patch 0302 already established for Curios' own Sell All (`runSellAllRecipes`,
+one `engine.sellRecipe` call per scroll type, `RECIPE_SELL_STAGGER_MS`
+apart, each with its own gold-fly particle) rather than one flat bulk
+call with zero feedback.
+
+**Locked-but-visible crafting list (`CraftingStation.tsx`).** Direct
+answer: "visible but locked -- shows what's out there to chase." Every
+recipe picker (the plain gear/gem list, the Alchemist-style consumable/
+charm table, and the Enchant tab's own sigil-tier picker) now marks an
+unlearned recipe `disabled` and swaps its sublabel to "🔒 Recipe not yet
+learned" instead of its usual description/material readout.
+`PickerOption.disabled` already existed and already fully blocked
+selection with a greyed `.disabled` CSS treatment (`opacity: 0.4`) --
+nothing new needed there, just finally used for this.
+
+**Existing saves (`SaveManager.ts`, migration 64, `SAVE_VERSION` ->
+65).** Every recipe was unconditionally craftable before this patch, so
+a save old enough to need migrating has, by definition, already had
+full access to all 72 -- migration grants literally every recipe id
+that exists (not just the 16 always-known ones a brand-new guild starts
+with), so nobody who already had it loses anything retroactively. A
+newly-introduced scroll simply has nothing left to grant for an
+already-migrated save if one somehow still drops -- `RecipeManager.learn`'s
+own "already known" guard makes that a harmless no-op.
+
+**Verified.** `npx tsc --noEmit` and `npx vite build --config
+vite.web.config.ts` both pass clean (206 modules, up from 203 -- the 3
+new files). Recipe/scroll data cross-checked by script: all 56 scroll
+`recipeId`s resolve against `crafting-recipes.json`, all 56 scroll `id`s
+are unique, and the 16 always-known + 56 gated partition covers all 72
+recipes with nothing left over or double-counted. No live in-app
+playtest in this environment -- worth a real pass confirming a fresh
+guild can actually craft its starting 16 recipes immediately, that a
+dropped scroll shows correctly in both result modals and the new
+Recipes section, and that an old save loads with every pre-existing
+recipe intact.
+
+
 ```discord-update
 Dev Update | Patch 0356
 
